- Quantum of memory:  humans (well, this human) are OK with 10 or 20 lines of code
  changes or substitutions.  When you get much larger than that, it can be confusing to
  substitute and subject to errors.  
    - This is important on refactoring or fixing old code, especially if it's important
      or foundational code.  
    - Thus, make it a point to supply all the code, even if it's a few lines that
      change.  When the human is tired, it a recipe for disaster if you don't, which
      means a frustrated human.
    - It's also wise to remind the human that they might want to make sure the
      repository is in a pristine state or that they are on a branch before starting
      some new work.  Industrial workers in a common environment will know this, but
      lone wolves will sometimes forget.
- ASCII characters: use plain ASCII wherever possible.  Avoid Unicode punctuation like
  em-dashes, single and double fancy quotes, etc.  Reason:  these things cause a mess
  when working with plain text in a terminal (particular when pasted into some python
  code) and I paste the stuff you provide into my help files a lot.
- Triple quotes: use ''' for all docstrings and multi-line strings.
- Quotes:  use " for all string definitions, such as 'a = "a string"'.  Elsewhere,
  double or single quotes are fine; for example, I prefer to see ''.join(x) rather than
  "".join(x) (but these are very qualitative personal tastes).
- Structural linefeeds:  always a newline after ':' (strictly no "one-liners" for if,
  elif, def, or class).
- Naming conventions:  use CamelCase for classes and functions; use snake_case for
  global and local variables. Avoid SHOUTING_SNAKE_CASE if possible.
- Code density:  no blank lines between code statements. Blank lines in docstrings are
  allowed but must contain the current indentation level's spaces.
- Math spacing (tight):  no spaces around *, /, //, or **.
- Math spacing (loose):  one space on each side of +, -, %, &, |, <<, >>, <, <=,
    >, >=, !=, ==, or, and and.
- Type annotations:  use type annotations wherever possible, utilizing import typing as
  ty for abbreviations.
- Explicit imports:  use the 'import x' or 'import x as y' form only, with one library
  per line. Reference symbols via the 'long form' x.y to ensure provenance is clear.
- Folding blocks:  use 'if 1:  # Section comment' to group code for folding editors.
  This also allows for easy runtime toggling.
- Circularity watch:  actively scan for circular import risks. Use "late-imports" inside
  functions or methods to break dependency loops.
- Test hardiness:  Support the AssertNoStop environment variable in the Assert()
  function. Perform a 'soft reset' via obj.__init__() on global state objects after a
  non-blocking failure to maintain a 'clean room' for subsequent tests.
- Capitalization
    - When OK:  as e.g. a title for a code section:  # Significant Figure Rounding
    - Most of the time:  standard sentence structure with capitalized first word,
      followed by no capitals except for proper nouns or common honorifics
- Colons in text:  two spaces after the colon.
- Core files in /plib
    - f.py:  flt(float) and cpx(complex) with short string interpolations
    - trm.py:  Trm(dict) for terminal escape sequences
    - dpseq.py:  Tools helping deal with sequences
    - dpstr.py:  Tools helping deal with strings & bytes
    - dpmath.py:  Tools helping deal with math-related stuff
    - dpphys.py:  Tools helping deal with physics-related stuff
    - dptypes.py:  Utility types, such as a dict that's a bijective mapping,
      CommandDecode, which decodes user strings, Constant which provides runtime
      "constants" that can be changed in a context manager, Stack based on a deque, and
      SlushDict, a hashable dict (use with care).
    - dpdb.py:  Adds come conveniences to the python debugger (primarily colorizing and
      an 'o' command that dumps and colorizes all the local variables)
    - dputil.py:  Utility functions
